# Geeboard's panel behind Caddy — the two ways a panel gets https.
#
# **You should not need this file.** `sudo bash deploy/linux/install-panel.sh`
# asks whether you have a domain, renders one of these two blocks from
# deploy/panel/caddy/panel.caddyfile.tmpl, writes /etc/caddy/Caddyfile,
# reloads Caddy, and — for the second case — leaves the certificate
# authority where a node installer can find it. This file is the reference
# for doing it by hand, which is docs/advanced-install.md.
#
#   sudo apt install caddy
#   sudo cp deploy/panel/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile   # keep one block
#   sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# The panel listens on 127.0.0.1:3000 and speaks plain HTTP (PANEL_BIND in
# deploy/panel/.env — change the address below to match if you move it). It
# is not published anywhere else: sessions are Secure cookies, so a panel
# reached over http:// cannot sign anybody in, and the certificate is this
# file's business.
#
# `flush_interval -1` is not decoration. The live console is a stream of
# server-sent events, and a proxy that buffers turns it into a page that
# never arrives.

# ── A name, and a certificate every browser already trusts ───────────
#
# Caddy gets it from Let's Encrypt and renews it. The name has to resolve
# to this machine, and ports 80 and 443 have to be open to the internet.
# PANEL_URL in deploy/panel/.env is https://panel.example.com.
#
# `tls you@example.com` is the address Let's Encrypt writes to when a
# certificate is about to expire and has not renewed. The installer asks
# for it; by hand it is optional, and worth setting.
#
# Nothing else to do: node agents trust this certificate the way your
# browser does.
panel.example.com {
	tls you@example.com
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
		flush_interval -1
	}
}

# ── An address, and a certificate authority of your own ──────────────
#
# No domain name, so no public authority will issue anything: `tls
# internal` makes Caddy its own authority and signs a certificate for
# this address with it. PANEL_URL is https://203.0.113.10.
#
# What that costs, and it is the whole of the difference: nothing trusts
# that authority yet. A browser says so once and you accept it; a node
# agent is a Node.js program with a trust store of public authorities and
# it will refuse the certificate, with UNABLE_TO_VERIFY_LEAF_SIGNATURE or
# SELF_SIGNED_CERT_IN_CHAIN under "registering with the panel failed".
#
# Give the agent the authority instead of taking the checking away. The
# panel writes this option into the command itself, because PANEL_URL is
# an address rather than a name:
#
#   sudo bash deploy/linux/install.sh https://203.0.113.10 'gbn_…' --panel-ca auto
#
# On the panel's own machine that is the whole command: `auto` is the
# authority as it sits on this machine. Elsewhere it is not here, and the
# installer says so — copy /etc/geeboard/panel-ca.crt over and pass
# `--panel-ca <that file>` instead.
# Either way it ends up at /etc/geeboard/panel-ca.crt on the node, with
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS pointing at it — one more authority trusted
# alongside the public ones, rather than no checking at all.
#
# docs/production.md, "No domain name", has the whole of it.
#
# 203.0.113.10 {
# 	tls internal
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
# 		flush_interval -1
# 	}
# }
